Hi all,
Hope it's okay that I jump on this thread - I have similar questions.

I ran a common factor model using the userGWAS function for reasons similar to Dan's. Some SNPs also still generated negative variance warnings (also, like Dan, this was a small proportion of the output).

For the independent pathways model, I did:

mod_indep <- 'F1 = ~ PHENO1 + PHENO2 + PHENO3
PHENO1 ~~ a*PHENO1
a > 0.001
PHENO2 ~~ b*PHENO2
b > 0.001
PHENO3 ~~ c* PHENO3
c > 0.001
PHENO1 ~ SNP
PHENO2 ~ SNP
PHENO3 ~ SNP'

indep <- userGWAS(covstruc = LDSCoutput, SNPs = split_sumstats, model = mod_indep, parallel = T, cores = 6, sub = c("PHENO1~SNP", "PHENO2~SNP", "PHENO3~SNP"))

All of the resulting chisq df and p-value values were 0, and I realized that the independent pathways model for a single factor model with 3 indicators is a perfect fit, and that I could probably just use the chisq values from the original model to compute the Q index. Is this correct?

The problem with this is that all of the chisq values in the original model are significant, which would mean that all of the SNPs are heterogeneous across the 3 indicators. Am I interpreting this correctly?

What is the most appropriate way to compute the Q index for common factor models run with the userGWAS() function?

Thanks so much,

Sarah

